  "comparativeKeyFindings": [
    {
      "title": "Why Effective Tax Rate is the Only Fair Comparison Metric",
      "detail": "In New Jersey, nominal/certified rates can be completely misleading. For example, Clifton's certified rate (6.527%) appears huge compared to Wayne (2.748%) or Parsippany (3.550%), but that is solely because Clifton assesses homes at ~33.3% of market value. On a true $600,000 market value home, Clifton's effective tax ($13,038) is comparable to Wayne ($13,254) and lower than Parsippany ($14,184)."
    },
    {
      "title": "Where The Money Goes: School Share vs. County Share Contrast",
      "detail": "Morris County towns (Parsippany, Montville, Mountain Lakes) send 64%\u201368% of property taxes to local schools because State Aid is under 10%, while Morris County takes less than 10%. Conversely, Passaic County towns (Clifton, Wayne) pay 15%\u201323% to Passaic County, while schools receive larger state allocations."
    },
    {
      "title": "Commercial Ratables & The PILOT Burden Shift",
      "detail": "Towns with heavy commercial and corporate ratables (like Hanover at 38.8% commercial and Morristown at 43.6%) achieve the lowest effective tax rates (1.49%\u20131.95%). Towns that lost corporate office parks or granted long-term residential PILOT exemptions shift millions onto single-family homeowners."
    }
  ]
